{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JfScRohW4k1QSdvc1W9vi7","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"capsaicin supplement clinical evidence energy expenditure vascular function meta-analysis\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Capsaicin and capsiate could be appropriate agents for treatment of obesity: A meta-analysis of human studies\",\"url\":\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28001433/\"},{\"title\":\"pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\",\"url\":\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33063385/\"},{\"title\":\"The effects of capsaicin and capsiate on energy balance: critical review and meta-analyses of studies in humans\",\"url\":\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22038945/\"},{\"title\":\"pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\",\"url\":\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41856833/\"},{\"title\":\"journal article\",\"url\":\"https://cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0007114523000697/type/journal_article\"},{\"title\":\"core.ac.uk\",\"url\":\"https://core.ac.uk/download/478821329.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Potential ergogenic aid of capsaicinoid or capsinoids in healthy adults: a systematic review with meta-analysis\",\"url\":\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41505853/\"},{\"title\":\"Capsaicinoid Ingestion, Human Metabolism and Exercise (Phase 2)\",\"url\":\"https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT02138630\"},{\"title\":\"Capsaicinoid Ingestion, Human Metabolism and Exercise\",\"url\":\"https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT02139852\"},{\"title\":\"effects of capsaicin intake on weight loss among overweight and obese subjects a systematic review and metaanalysis of randomized controlled trials\",\"url\":\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/AF1C3A4331A35BA12CE925B0B56818B8/S0007114523000697a.pdf/effects_of_capsaicin_intake_on_weight_loss_among_overweight_and_obese_subjects_a_systematic_review_and_metaanalysis_of_randomized_controlled_trials.pdf\"}]\n\nThe search turned up good energy-expenditure evidence but little direct vascular evidence.\n\n**Energy expenditure:** Several meta-analyses report a modest metabolic effect. A 2012 review concluded that capsaicin and capsiate increase energy expenditure and fat oxidation, but the magnitude of these effects is small. A 2021 meta-analysis of 13 placebo-controlled trials in healthy adults found a pooled increase in resting metabolic rate of about 34 kcal/day, along with a lower respiratory quotient, which indicates more fat burning. A 2018 meta-analysis found the energy-expenditure effect appeared only in studies with participants above a BMI of 25 kg/m².\n\n**Vascular function:** The most direct evidence is a 2026 systematic review and meta-analysis of cardiovascular risk factors. It found that red pepper/capsaicin supplementation may yield modest benefits in reducing total cholesterol and diastolic blood pressure, but sensitivity analysis showed the significant results for TC and DBP depended on a single study. The authors called for larger, longer RCTs. A separate trial registry entry notes that more in depth investigations of the effects on endothelial function, vascular autonomic tone and inflammation are lacking. I did not find a dedicated meta-analysis of endothelial or arterial function, so the vascular evidence is thin.\n\n**Caveat:** I didn't check the full text of these studies, so these are summaries of abstracts and search snippets, not a full appraisal of study quality or dosing.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1ae1b95f-0a75-4473-b2e5-cff7fb3dca41","uuid":"d6e1d8e4-6309-4133-82b5-6cc695d83f52","timestamp":"2026-11-24T14:01:21.299Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"capsaicin supplement clinical evidence energy expenditure vascular function meta-analysis","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_011rjRtfnE7mNCL7vbyCQ1tX","content":[{"title":"Capsaicin and capsiate could be appropriate agents for treatment of obesity: 