which indicates more fat burning. A 2018 meta-analysis found the energy-expenditure effect appeared only in studies with participants above a BMI of 25 kg/m².\n\n**Vascular function:** The most direct evidence is a 2026 systematic review and meta-analysis of cardiovascular risk factors. It found that red pepper/capsaicin supplementation may yield modest benefits in reducing total cholesterol and diastolic blood pressure, but sensitivity analysis showed the significant results for TC and DBP depended on a single study. The authors called for larger, longer RCTs. A separate trial registry entry notes that more in depth investigations of the effects on endothelial function, vascular autonomic tone and inflammation are lacking. I did not find a dedicated meta-analysis of endothelial or arterial function, so the vascular evidence is thin.\n\n**Caveat:** I didn't check the full text of these studies, so these are summaries of abstracts and search snippets, not a full appraisal of study quality or dosing."],"durationSeconds":6.706401540999999,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01JfScRohW4k1QSdvc1W9vi7","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
